{
  "language": "javascript",
  "case": "surface_forms",
  "why": "One shape per rule of the CommonJS export tiers (js-review-followup D5: every rule moves a gated corpus metric, not only a unit test). A `Reflect.defineProperty` write and a write inside a declaration (`var all = exports.list = list`) are exports; top-level `this.find =` writes `exports`; `__proto__` in the exported literal and `Object.create` of a local prototype make members callable that the file never writes, so those modules are resolved by name; a getter is an export whose value this pass cannot name, resolved by name; `exports.stale` after `module.exports = make()` is written onto an object nobody exports. `util.util()` through `const util = require('./util')` is the member `util`, not the whole module. And a `var` in a class expression's `static {}` block is the block's own. Each `secret` is declared and never exported. The second review round added: a write under a top-level branch *before* `module.exports` is replaced, and one inside a function, which runs later and is live; a top-level block's own `const api`; four spellings the reference finder once missed \u2014 `{ exports }`, `const mod = module`, `module['exports']`, `exports.__proto__ =` \u2014 each of which must make the surface opaque rather than read a map that lacks `g`; `var exports = {}`, a rebinding; a write after `Object.freeze`; a `catch` parameter spelled like the alias; and `this` inside a top-level arrow, which is still `exports`.",
